Description: Depth: Give a window's insides a range of their own, placed as KWin stacks
 .
 The user's sunk and popped limits are for the desktop's own elements: windows,
 menus, popups, tooltips and dialogs. Inside a window they buried a text field
 ten pixels deep. Elements inside a window or a popup now use an element range
 of about a pixel each way, relative to the surface they lie on (ElementLimit in
 kwinrc's [StereoDepth] group, 1 pixel by default); menus, popups, tooltips and
 dialogs drawn inside a window keep the user's limits.
 .
 In both ranges the levels follow the rule Stereo KWin uses for the desktop's
 surfaces, StereoDepth::level() in KWin's src/scene/stereodepth.cpp:
 .
 - Nesting is a stack, as KWin's popups are (Role::Popped): each element steps
   out from what it lies on, or back, and where the stack goes beyond a limit
   that side of the surface is scaled until the farthest sits at the limit, so
   equal steps become 1/N .. N/N. A desktop element is never behind the one it
   was opened from, and a popped element at the output's edge stays where it
   lies (touchesEdge()).
 - A group with one current item is KWin's pile of windows (Role::Sunk): the
   current tab, row, page or checked segment lies where its group lies, as the
   active window sits at the screen, and the others step back by how far they
   are from it, the farthest at the limit. The current tab no longer sinks.
 - Parallaxes are rounded and split between the eyes as StereoDepth::parallax()
   and StereoDepth::eyeShift() do. Qt Quick receives the unrounded parallax,
   the right eye moving by whole pixels and the left eye by the rest.
 .
 The two KWin fittings are one rule: a side's range is shared by what is
 stacked on it, the farthest at the limit. They differ only in which end is
 anchored (the topmost popup at the popped limit, the lowest window at the sunk
 limit), in the opener rule, which only popups have, and in the edge rule, which
 only popped surfaces need.
 .
 The theme decides an element's sign and size: an outer shadow raises it and
 an inset one sinks it, by its strength, opacity * (length(offset) + blur / 2 +
 spread) in the terms of CSS Backgrounds and Borders 3 section 6.1, the
 strongest shadow on each side of a surface being one whole step. A theme with
 heavy shadows everywhere therefore keeps its order without pushing everything
 to the limit, and one that sinks what others raise keeps its order. A declared
 depth still wins. Breeze's depth.css loses its state table (checked buttons,
 the current tab and selected rows sinking, disabled controls at the screen):
 Breeze draws those without a shadow, so they lie flat.
 .
 Every element now carries its parallax, at the window's plane too, so a widget
 without one can move with the widget it is in; and a sunk Qt Quick item that
 draws itself, such as a text field drawing its text, moves whole instead of
 leaving its text behind.
